{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head b966b0f499aace6120f9c2ff01ab48211e59d489 on repair/performance-instrumentation-review, pushed to origin. Artifacts: c053cf8ce4d462380359523bd8996331874da3d4 at internal/observe, e4a810d026d10f46d5c74554198835b48934b936 at internal/telemetry, ebe1cff2f9953a78e3cc41c611f78aa95c190d6e at docs/reference/gs/serve.md.\n\nWHAT CARRIED FORWARD UNCHANGED. The reviewed implementation was cherry-picked onto current main as aba129e4 and I verified the trees are byte-identical to 154d60127f before touching anything, so the architecture, the default-off behaviour, the loopback profiler policy and the exporter-neutral seam are the ones already reviewed rather than a re-implementation. No production code changed in this repair. The whole diff on top is one documentation page and two test files.\n\nFINDING 1, THE GATE. docs/reference/gs/serve.md now enumerates --otel-endpoint and --profile-listen and describes them: that off means absent rather than quiet, that HTTP labels carry the registered route template and never the request path, that Go runtime metrics are included, and that the profiler binds its own checked loopback address with the caution that reaching that port means reading a memory profile. TestGateSurfaceCoversEveryCLISubcommand passes.\n\nFINDING 2, THE UNHELD BOUND. TestRouteTemplateIsBounded covers a registered template, the empty pattern, a disallowed character, a percent-encoded byte, a non-ASCII byte, and both sides of the 96-byte ceiling. Pinning both sides was deliberate: a test that only tries a wildly over-length pattern still passes when someone raises the limit, and I proved that by raising it to 4096 and watching a single-case version stay green.\n\nFINDING 3, THE VACUOUS ASSERTION. TestInMemoryExporterGetsTemplateOnlyHTTPMetrics now walks span event attributes and every collected metric datapoint attribute, which is where Record actually puts them, instead of span.Attributes, which no operation attribute ever reaches. It counts what it inspected and requires the bounded http.route to be present in both places, so a walk that found nothing fails instead of passing quietly.\n\nMUTATIONS RUN AGAINST THIS EXACT HEAD, EACH RESTORED AFTER. Deleting the route guard fails TestRouteTemplateIsBounded. Raising only the ceiling to 4096 fails it too. Routing the raw URL path into the observer now fails both internal/observe and internal/telemetry — at the reviewed head it failed only internal/observe, which is the whole of finding 3. Suppressing the route attributes entirely fails the vacuity guard.\n\nGATES AT THIS HEAD. git diff --check clean; go build clean; go vet clean; go test -race on internal/observe and internal/telemetry pass; an uncached internal/docset run passes in 82s; the full go test ./... passes with no failures.\n\nONE PROCESS NOTE, RECORDED BECAUSE IT NEARLY COST THE WORK. My first mutation round restored files with git checkout -- internal/, which reverted the mutation and the new uncommitted tests together, so the three later mutations ran against tests that no longer existed and all reported green. I noticed because three mutations that should have failed did not, checked whether the edits were landing rather than trusting the result, and found my own tests gone. The repair head was committed before the second round, so every mutation result above was taken against committed work. I am stating it because a green mutation round is exactly the thing that looks like evidence when it is nothing.\n\nThe three findings I raised are closed. I am not reviewing this; the request asks for fresh cross-actor review and I am requesting it separately.","body":{"branch":"repair/performance-instrumentation-review","head":"b966b0f499aace6120f9c2ff01ab48211e59d489","status":"ready-for-review"}}